jellyfin-hotcache for Debian/Ubuntu
-----------------------------------

Configuration is deliberately operator-owned.

The package installs:

  /usr/sbin/jellyfin-hotcache
  /usr/share/jellyfin-hotcache/config.yml.example
  systemd service and timer units

The package creates the directory:

  /etc/jellyfin-hotcache/

The package does NOT install or overwrite:

  /etc/jellyfin-hotcache/config.yml
  /etc/jellyfin-hotcache/environment

To configure a new installation:

  cp /usr/share/jellyfin-hotcache/config.yml.example \
     /etc/jellyfin-hotcache/config.yml
  chmod 0600 /etc/jellyfin-hotcache/config.yml

Put secrets such as JELLYFIN_API_KEY in the optional environment file rather
than embedding them in config.yml where practical.

Run the read-only preflight before enabling mutation:

  jellyfin-hotcache --check

The timer is not automatically enabled by package installation. After a clean
preflight and after reviewing dry-run behavior:

  systemctl enable --now jellyfin-hotcache.timer

Site-specific mount ordering should be supplied with a local systemd drop-in,
not by modifying the package-managed unit.

Legacy manual installations
---------------------------

Older installations may have files at:

  /usr/local/sbin/jellyfin-hotcache
  /etc/jellyfin-hotcache.yml
  /etc/systemd/system/jellyfin-hotcache.service
  /etc/systemd/system/jellyfin-hotcache.timer

The package does not delete or overwrite those files. A unit in
/etc/systemd/system takes precedence over the package unit in
/usr/lib/systemd/system, and /usr/local/sbin may precede /usr/sbin in root's
PATH. Migrate the old configuration to /etc/jellyfin-hotcache/config.yml, move
secrets to /etc/jellyfin-hotcache/environment where practical, run --check,
and then remove or archive the legacy executable and unit files. Run
`systemctl daemon-reload` after removing legacy units.
